    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>Timing matters: help is most useful before the insurer’s inspection, before permanent repairs, and before you accept a final payment or sign a release.</li>
<li>Common signals to call include large losses, an estimate far below contractor bids, partial or full denials, and claims that stall past Texas Prompt Payment timelines.</li>
<li>Small, clearly covered claims that are paid fairly often do not need a public adjuster.</li>
<li>Texas caps public adjuster fees at 10% of the settlement and gives you 72 hours to cancel the contract.</li>
<li>Legal questions, such as lawsuits or deadlines to sue, belong with a Texas attorney.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>Most property owners never plan to hire anyone for an insurance claim. They report the damage, wait for the adjuster, and expect a check. Sometimes that is exactly how it goes. Other times, a few weeks in, the estimate is thin, the calls go unanswered, or a letter arrives citing an exclusion nobody mentioned. <strong>Knowing when to call a public adjuster</strong> is about recognizing those turning points early enough to do something useful about them.</p>

<p>This guide is for homeowners and business owners in Dallas, Dallas County, and nearby North Texas cities. It walks through each stage of a typical claim, the warning signs that suggest outside help may be worth it, the situations where you probably do not need it, and what to have ready if you decide to make the call.</p>

<h2>A quick refresher: who the public adjuster works for</h2>

<p>A public adjuster is licensed by the Texas Department of Insurance under <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.4102.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Insurance Code Chapter 4102</a> and represents the policyholder. The adjuster your insurer sends, whether a staff employee or an independent adjuster on contract, evaluates the claim for the insurance company. Both can be professional and fair; they simply have different clients. If you want the full background first, read <a href="/what-is-a-public-claims-adjuster/">what is a public claims adjuster</a>.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/The-Key-Differences-Between-Adjuster-Types-1024x683.webp" alt="Three adjusters in a safety vest, a work shirt, and a suit holding clipboards in front of a brick house with a tarped roof" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Several adjusters may look at the same home. Only a public adjuster represents the owner.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>When to call a public adjuster: the claim timeline</h2>

<p>The table below shows where a claim usually stands at each stage, what can go wrong, and how useful a call tends to be. It is a guide, not a rule; every policy and loss is different.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Claim stage</th><th>What can go wrong</th><th>Is it a good time to call?</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Right after the loss</td><td>Evidence fades, temporary repairs go undocumented, damage keeps spreading</td><td>Yes, for large or complex losses</td></tr>
<tr><td>Before the insurer’s inspection</td><td>A short inspection misses rooms, roof slopes, or hidden moisture</td><td>Yes, especially for roof, water, and fire claims</td></tr>
<tr><td>After the first estimate arrives</td><td>Missing line items, low quantities, wrong materials, repair instead of replace</td><td>Yes; a common and practical checkpoint</td></tr>
<tr><td>After a partial or full denial</td><td>Cause of loss blamed on wear, age, or an exclusion</td><td>Yes, to review the evidence and policy language</td></tr>
<tr><td>Claim stalled for weeks</td><td>Repeated document requests, adjuster turnover, no decision</td><td>Often</td></tr>
<tr><td>During repairs</td><td>Contractors uncover hidden damage that is not in the estimate</td><td>Often, for supplements</td></tr>
<tr><td>After final payment and a signed release</td><td>Reopening may be limited by the release and policy terms</td><td>Harder; read what you signed and consider an attorney</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Right after major damage</h3>
<p>A house fire, a tornado, a tree through the roof, or a burst pipe that soaks several rooms can turn into a claim with many parts: structure, contents, additional living expenses, debris removal, and sometimes code-related work. On losses like these, early involvement lets the damage be documented before cleanup and drying change what can be seen. Dallas has seen plenty of these events, from the December 26, 2015 EF4 tornado through Garland and Rowlett to the October 20, 2019 EF3 tornado in north Dallas and the pipe failures that followed Winter Storm Uri in February 2021.</p>

<h3>Before the insurer’s inspection</h3>
<p>The first inspection sets the tone for the claim. If the adjuster only walks one side of the roof, skips the attic, or does not check soft metals and the AC unit after a hailstorm, the estimate will reflect that. Having your own documentation ready, or a representative present, helps make sure everything is seen. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> lists what to gather.</p>

<h3>When the estimate seems low</h3>
<p>This is the moment most people start searching for help. You have the insurer’s estimate in one hand and a contractor’s bid in the other, and they are far apart. Signs the estimate may be short include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Rooms, elevations, or roof slopes that are not in the estimate at all</li>
<li>Repair line items where replacement is needed (for example, spot shingle repair on a roof with damage across every slope)</li>
<li>Missing components such as drip edge, starter, ridge cap, flashing, or code-required items</li>
<li>Low quantities for drywall, flooring, or paint, or no line for matching adjoining areas where the policy supports it</li>
<li>Depreciation that seems high for the age and condition of the materials</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> and <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid insurance claims</a> pages explain how a line-by-line comparison works.</p>

<h3>When the claim is denied</h3>
<p>Denials often cite wear and tear, pre-existing damage, improper maintenance, or an exclusion such as flood or long-term leakage. Some denials are correct under the policy. Others come down to how clearly the cause and date of loss were documented. A public adjuster can review the denial letter, the policy wording, and the evidence, and help you decide whether there is a basis to ask the insurer to reconsider. See <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied insurance claims</a> for more.</p>

[[cta]]

<h3>When the claim stalls</h3>
<p>Texas’s <a href="https://statutes.capitol.texas.gov/Docs/IN/htm/IN.542.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prompt Payment of Claims law</a> gives insurers deadlines. In general, the insurer has 15 days after notice to acknowledge the claim, begin its investigation, and request the information it needs; 15 business days after receiving everything it requested to accept or reject the claim, or to notify you that it needs more time, after which it has up to 45 more days; and 5 business days after notifying you of acceptance to pay. After a weather-related catastrophe declared by the insurance commissioner, these deadlines can be extended by 15 days. Eligible surplus lines insurers follow different timeframes.</p>
<p>A few slow weeks after a big storm is common. Repeated requests for the same documents, multiple adjuster changes, or months without a decision are a different matter. Our <a href="/services/delayed-insurance-claims/">delayed insurance claims</a> page covers what you can do, and TDI also accepts consumer complaints.</p>

<h3>During repairs</h3>
<p>Contractors often find damage nobody could see at inspection: wet decking under shingles, rotted framing behind siding, or water in wall cavities. That additional damage usually needs to be reported to the insurer, documented, and added to the claim through a supplement before the work is covered up. If you are already mid-repair and the estimate keeps falling short, that is a reasonable time to call.</p>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/Benefits-of-Hiring-a-Public-Adjuster-1024x683.webp" alt="Adjuster with a laptop reviewing papers with a couple at a dining table" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>A review of the policy and the insurer’s estimate is often the first step in deciding whether help is worthwhile.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Why waiting too long can hurt</h2>

<p>There is rarely a single moment when it becomes “too late,” but options narrow over time:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Evidence disappears.</strong> Hail marks weather, water dries, and damaged materials are hauled off. Photos taken weeks later may not show what was there.</li>
<li><strong>Repairs cover the damage.</strong> Once new drywall or a new roof is installed, it is harder to prove what the old materials looked like.</li>
<li><strong>Releases and final payments.</strong> Signing a release or accepting a payment described as final can limit what can be reopened. Read anything you sign.</li>
<li><strong>Policy deadlines.</strong> Many policies set time limits for reporting damage, completing repairs to recover depreciation, and filing suit. Many Texas policies include a contractual deadline to sue, often two years and one day from when the claim accrued. Check your policy and talk to an attorney about anything involving a lawsuit.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When you probably do not need a public adjuster</h2>

<p>Not every claim needs representation, and saying so is part of giving honest advice. You can often handle it yourself when:</p>
<ul>
<li>The damage is limited and clearly covered, such as a small, sudden leak with a clear cause</li>
<li>The insurer’s estimate is in line with written contractor bids</li>
<li>Payment arrives promptly and the adjuster answers your questions</li>
<li>The total loss is close to your deductible, so the potential difference is small</li>
</ul>
<p>Remember that many Dallas policies carry a separate wind/hail deductible, often a percentage of the dwelling (Coverage A) limit. Check your declarations page so you know how much of the loss you will carry regardless.</p>

<h2>Claim types where an early call is common</h2>

<h3>Hail and wind</h3>
<p>Dallas County hail reports peak in April and May, with a long March-to-June season and a smaller rise in October, according to NOAA storm records. Roof claims involve many components and frequent disagreement over repair versus replacement. See <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">hail damage claims</a>.</p>

<h3>Fire and smoke</h3>
<p>Fire claims combine structure, smoke and soot, water from firefighting, contents, and living expenses. The contents inventory alone can take weeks. Our article on the <a href="/fire-insurance-adjuster/">fire insurance adjuster</a> goes into more detail.</p>

<h3>Water and freeze damage</h3>
<p>Water moves into subfloors, cabinets, and wall cavities quickly, and older pier-and-beam homes in areas like Lakewood, the M Streets, and Oak Cliff can hide damage beneath the floor. Policies often treat sudden discharge differently from long-term seepage, so the cause matters.</p>

<h3>Commercial property</h3>
<p>Larger policies, low-slope roofing systems, tenants, and business income coverage add layers. Owners often want help early simply because their time is better spent running the business.</p>

<h2>What to have ready when you call</h2>

<ul>
<li>Your full policy, including the declarations page and endorsements</li>
<li>The claim number and the insurer’s adjuster contact information</li>
<li>Photos and video of the damage, with dates</li>
<li>Any estimates, letters, or emails from the insurer</li>
<li>Contractor bids and receipts for temporary repairs</li>
<li>A short timeline: date of loss, date reported, inspection dates, and key conversations</li>
</ul>

<h2>Questions to ask before you hire</h2>

<ul>
<li>Are you licensed by TDI, and what is your license number?</li>
<li>What is your fee, and is it written in the contract? (Texas caps it at 10% of the settlement.)</li>
<li>How will you document and estimate my claim?</li>
<li>Who will I communicate with, and how often will I get updates?</li>
<li>Do you have experience with this type of loss?</li>
</ul>
<p>At Dallas Public Adjuster, we welcome these questions. A reputable public adjuster will not pressure you to sign on the spot, will not promise a specific payout, and will not offer to do the repairs. Texas rules prohibit a public adjuster from taking part in the repair of a property on a claim they are adjusting.</p>

<h2>Final thoughts</h2>

<p>The right time to call a public adjuster is usually earlier than people think, but not every claim needs one. If your loss is large, the estimate does not match the damage, the claim was denied, or it has stopped moving, it is reasonable to get an independent look before you accept a final number. Dallas Public Adjuster serves Dallas, Dallas County, and surrounding North Texas communities, and a <a href="/free-claim-review/">free claim review</a> can help you decide whether representation makes sense for your situation. For questions about lawsuits or legal deadlines, speak with a Texas attorney.</p>
